    "text": "To determine how much weight Tom can now lift, we need to follow the steps of his training and calculate the final weight he can hold.\n\n1. **Initial Weight Capacity:**\n   Tom originally could lift 80 kg per hand. Since he can lift with both hands, his initial total weight capacity is:\n   \\[\n   80 \\text{ kg/hand} \\times 2 \\text{ hands} = 160 \\text{ kg}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Double the Initial Weight:**\n   After training, Tom doubled his initial weight capacity. So, his new weight capacity is:\n   \\[\n   160 \\text{ kg} \\times 2 = 320 \\text{ kg}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Increase by 10%:**\n   Tom then decided to specialize and increased his weight capacity by 10%. To find out how much this increase is, we calculate 10% of 320 kg:\n   \\[\n   320 \\text{ kg} \\times 0.10 = 32 \\text{ kg}\n   \\]\n   Adding this increase to his new weight capacity gives:\n   \\[\n   320 \\text{ kg} + 32 \\text{ kg} = 352 \\text{ kg}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the total weight Tom can now hold is \\(\\boxed{352}\\) kg.",
